  "content": {
    "id": "p14-cc-8-response",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "8",
    "prompt": "Bob is asked to differentiate $f(x)=10$ using first principles. He claims that it is not possible because there is no expression to substitute $x+h$ into, and therefore there is no such thing as a derivative function in this case. Is Bob correct?",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "No. The graph is horizontal, so $f'(x)=0$.",
      "worked": "No. Since $f(x+h)=10$, \n\n$$\\begin{align*}f'(x)&=\\lim_{h\\to0}\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h}\\\\f'(x)&=\\lim_{h\\to0}\\frac{10-10}{h}\\\\f'(x)&=0.\\end{align*}$$\n\n",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 30
  },
